INSERT INTO posts(slug,title,description,category_slug,body_html,body_markdown,cover_path,author,updated_at,reading_minutes,toc_json,status,sort_order) VALUES ('how-to-create-a-digital-product-from-scratch-(2026-step-by-step-guide)','Create a Digital Product from Scratch: From Problem to First Sale','Start with a problem you understand well enough to solve. A small complete product is easier to validate, deliver and improve than a broad promise with months of production behind it.','ideas-growth','<p class="site-lead">Start with a problem you understand well enough to solve. A small complete product is easier to validate, deliver and improve than a broad promise with months of production behind it.</p><h2 id="section-1">Define the outcome</h2><p>Write one sentence describing who the product is for and what they can do after using it. Exclude needs outside that scope. This sentence should guide the contents, product page and support boundaries.</p><h2 id="section-2">Create the smallest useful version</h2><p>For a guide, draft the actionable chapter before designing the cover. For a template, test the workflow with realistic sample data. For a course, record one complete lesson and its exercise before producing the full library.</p><h2 id="section-3">Get feedback from the intended buyer</h2><p>Ask testers to use the product without you narrating every step. Record where they hesitate, what they expected and what they could not open. Fix the biggest usability gap before adding more material.</p><h2 id="section-4">Launch a clear offer</h2><p>Show a sample, exact contents, compatibility, licence terms and delivery timing. Choose standard, custom or scheduled fulfilment to match reality. Treat early support questions as evidence for the next revision, not as a reason to promise everything.</p><h2 id="action-plan">Your practical action plan</h2><ol><li><strong>Define the test.</strong> Choose one audience, one outcome and a small complete product. Write down what result would justify another version before spending on a larger launch.</li><li><strong>Make the offer reviewable.</strong> Show a sample and state what is included, what it requires and when it arrives. If taking preorders, make the delay explicit and test the scheduled delivery settings.</li><li><strong>Review real results.</strong> Use purchases, contribution after costs and customer feedback. Revise the offer or stop the experiment if the evidence does not support expanding it.</li></ol><h2 id="before-launch">Before you publish</h2><div class="site-highlight"><ul><li>The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.</li><li>A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.</li><li>The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.</li><li>Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.</li><li>Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.</li></ul></div><h2 id="where-big-fits">Where BIG fits</h2><p>BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.</p><p><a href="/features">Explore the features</a>, <a href="/#plans">check the plans</a> or <a href="/tools">prepare your next launch with the free tools</a>.</p>','Start with a problem you understand well enough to solve. A small complete product is easier to validate, deliver and improve than a broad promise with months of production behind it.
## Define the outcome
Write one sentence describing who the product is for and what they can do after using it. Exclude needs outside that scope. This sentence should guide the contents, product page and support boundaries.
## Create the smallest useful version
For a guide, draft the actionable chapter before designing the cover. For a template, test the workflow with realistic sample data. For a course, record one complete lesson and its exercise before producing the full library.
## Get feedback from the intended buyer
Ask testers to use the product without you narrating every step. Record where they hesitate, what they expected and what they could not open. Fix the biggest usability gap before adding more material.
## Launch a clear offer
Show a sample, exact contents, compatibility, licence terms and delivery timing. Choose standard, custom or scheduled fulfilment to match reality. Treat early support questions as evidence for the next revision, not as a reason to promise everything.
## Your practical action plan
-
Define the test.
Choose one audience, one outcome and a small complete product. Write down what result would justify another version before spending on a larger launch.
-
Make the offer reviewable.
Show a sample and state what is included, what it requires and when it arrives. If taking preorders, make the delay explicit and test the scheduled delivery settings.
-
Review real results.
Use purchases, contribution after costs and customer feedback. Revise the offer or stop the experiment if the evidence does not support expanding it.
## Before you publish
-
The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.
-
A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.
-
The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.
-
Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.
-
Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.
## Where BIG fits
BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.
