INSERT INTO posts(slug,title,description,category_slug,body_html,body_markdown,cover_path,author,updated_at,reading_minutes,toc_json,status,sort_order) VALUES ('how-to-make-money-world-cup-2026-digital-products-shopify','Digital Products After the 2026 World Cup: What to Reuse Next','The 2026 tournament is over. A useful seasonal catalogue now needs a post-event plan: remove outdated promises, learn from actual buyer behaviour and adapt original designs for future events.','ideas-growth','<p class="site-lead">The 2026 tournament is over. A useful seasonal catalogue now needs a post-event plan: remove outdated promises, learn from actual buyer behaviour and adapt original designs for future events.</p><h2 id="section-1">Audit dated products</h2><p>Check dates, fixtures and headlines before running another promotion. Archive products that only make sense during the completed tournament, or describe them honestly as commemorative material. Do not advertise an upcoming match that has already happened.</p><h2 id="section-2">Reuse the structure, not protected branding</h2><p>An original match journal, generic scorecard or watch-party checklist can become an evergreen sports product. Review rights before using competition marks, team badges, player images or broadcast assets.</p><h2 id="section-3">Look at your own results</h2><p>Review product-page visits, purchases, support questions and refund reasons. A popular social post does not tell you whether the product sold profitably. Keep the format that helped buyers and revise the parts they found confusing.</p><h2 id="section-4">Build a future-event workflow</h2><p>Separate the reusable layout from dates and event data. Keep a source checklist for fixtures, a date for final review and a test print. This makes the next seasonal update more deliberate than changing only the year in a headline.</p><h2 id="action-plan">Your practical action plan</h2><ol><li><strong>Define the test.</strong> Choose one audience, one outcome and a small complete product. Write down what result would justify another version before spending on a larger launch.</li><li><strong>Make the offer reviewable.</strong> Show a sample and state what is included, what it requires and when it arrives. If taking preorders, make the delay explicit and test the scheduled delivery settings.</li><li><strong>Review real results.</strong> Use purchases, contribution after costs and customer feedback. Revise the offer or stop the experiment if the evidence does not support expanding it.</li></ol><h2 id="before-launch">Before you publish</h2><div class="site-highlight"><ul><li>The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.</li><li>A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.</li><li>The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.</li><li>Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.</li><li>Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.</li></ul></div><h2 id="where-big-fits">Where BIG fits</h2><p>BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.</p><p><a href="/features">Explore the features</a>, <a href="/#plans">check the plans</a> or <a href="/tools">prepare your next launch with the free tools</a>.</p>','The 2026 tournament is over. A useful seasonal catalogue now needs a post-event plan: remove outdated promises, learn from actual buyer behaviour and adapt original designs for future events.
## Audit dated products
Check dates, fixtures and headlines before running another promotion. Archive products that only make sense during the completed tournament, or describe them honestly as commemorative material. Do not advertise an upcoming match that has already happened.
## Reuse the structure, not protected branding
An original match journal, generic scorecard or watch-party checklist can become an evergreen sports product. Review rights before using competition marks, team badges, player images or broadcast assets.
## Look at your own results
Review product-page visits, purchases, support questions and refund reasons. A popular social post does not tell you whether the product sold profitably. Keep the format that helped buyers and revise the parts they found confusing.
## Build a future-event workflow
Separate the reusable layout from dates and event data. Keep a source checklist for fixtures, a date for final review and a test print. This makes the next seasonal update more deliberate than changing only the year in a headline.
## Your practical action plan
-
Define the test.
Choose one audience, one outcome and a small complete product. Write down what result would justify another version before spending on a larger launch.
-
Make the offer reviewable.
Show a sample and state what is included, what it requires and when it arrives. If taking preorders, make the delay explicit and test the scheduled delivery settings.
-
Review real results.
Use purchases, contribution after costs and customer feedback. Revise the offer or stop the experiment if the evidence does not support expanding it.
## Before you publish
-
The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.
-
A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.
-
The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.
-
Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.
-
Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.
## Where BIG fits
BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.
[Explore the features](https://www.bigdigitaldownload.com/features)
,
[check the plans](https://www.bigdigitaldownload.com/#plans)
or
[prepare your next launch with the free tools](https://www.bigdigitaldownload.com/tools)
.','/assets/editorial/blog--how-to-make-money-world-cup-2026-digital-products-shopify.webp','The BIG team','2026-09-22','3','[["section-1", "Audit dated products"], ["section-2", "Reuse the structure, not protected branding"], ["section-3", "Look at your own results"], ["section-4", "Build a future-event workflow"], ["action-plan", "Your practical action plan"], ["before-launch", "Before you publish"]]','published','16');
