INSERT INTO posts(slug,title,description,category_slug,body_html,body_markdown,cover_path,author,updated_at,reading_minutes,toc_json,status,sort_order) VALUES ('how-to-protect-digital-products-from-piracy-on-shopify-(5-layers)','Protect Digital Products on Shopify: Five Practical Layers','You can reduce unauthorised sharing, but no downloadable file is impossible to copy. Combine sensible access controls with a purchase experience that legitimate customers can use easily.','delivery','<p class="site-lead">You can reduce unauthorised sharing, but no downloadable file is impossible to copy. Combine sensible access controls with a purchase experience that legitimate customers can use easily.</p><h2 id="section-1">Layers 1 and 2: clear rights and controlled access</h2><p>State the licence scope before checkout and include it in the download package. Deliver through the app’s purchase-based access route rather than a public cloud link that anyone can forward. Avoid collecting unnecessary personal data for protection.</p><h2 id="section-2">Layer 3: reasonable download limits</h2><p>Set enough attempts for a failed connection and a second device. Too strict a limit creates support work without preventing someone from copying an already-downloaded file. Test how a legitimate buyer requests renewed access.</p><h2 id="section-3">Layer 4: PDF stamping</h2><p>A buyer-specific watermark can discourage casual redistribution. BIG’s documented stamping workflow uses an unprotected PDF. Test small pages, dark backgrounds and diagrams so the stamp does not damage the product.</p><h2 id="section-4">Layer 5: an incident response</h2><p>Keep evidence of your original work and the unauthorised distribution you find. Follow the relevant platform’s reporting process and review the facts before making a claim. Improve controls where useful without describing any app as an absolute piracy guarantee.</p><h2 id="action-plan">Your practical action plan</h2><ol><li><strong>Prepare the deliverable.</strong> Keep an original backup and publish a clear version. List the formats, compatibility and licence scope before the customer purchases.</li><li><strong>Connect and test in Shopify.</strong> Map the file, link or key to the right product and variant. Use the appropriate standard, custom or scheduled delivery mode. Check a controlled purchase on desktop and mobile.</li><li><strong>Choose capacity and monitor it.</strong> Use catalogue size and expected monthly transfer to choose a plan. Track actual usage and support questions after launch, especially before a promotion.</li></ol><h2 id="before-launch">Before you publish</h2><div class="site-highlight"><ul><li>The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.</li><li>A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.</li><li>The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.</li><li>Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.</li><li>Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.</li></ul></div><h2 id="where-big-fits">Where BIG fits</h2><p>BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.</p><p><a href="/features">Explore the features</a>, <a href="/#plans">check the plans</a> or <a href="/tools">prepare your next launch with the free tools</a>.</p>','You can reduce unauthorised sharing, but no downloadable file is impossible to copy. Combine sensible access controls with a purchase experience that legitimate customers can use easily.
## Layers 1 and 2: clear rights and controlled access
State the licence scope before checkout and include it in the download package. Deliver through the app’s purchase-based access route rather than a public cloud link that anyone can forward. Avoid collecting unnecessary personal data for protection.
## Layer 3: reasonable download limits
Set enough attempts for a failed connection and a second device. Too strict a limit creates support work without preventing someone from copying an already-downloaded file. Test how a legitimate buyer requests renewed access.
## Layer 4: PDF stamping
A buyer-specific watermark can discourage casual redistribution. BIG’s documented stamping workflow uses an unprotected PDF. Test small pages, dark backgrounds and diagrams so the stamp does not damage the product.
## Layer 5: an incident response
Keep evidence of your original work and the unauthorised distribution you find. Follow the relevant platform’s reporting process and review the facts before making a claim. Improve controls where useful without describing any app as an absolute piracy guarantee.
## Your practical action plan
-
Prepare the deliverable.
Keep an original backup and publish a clear version. List the formats, compatibility and licence scope before the customer purchases.
-
Connect and test in Shopify.
Map the file, link or key to the right product and variant. Use the appropriate standard, custom or scheduled delivery mode. Check a controlled purchase on desktop and mobile.
-
Choose capacity and monitor it.
Use catalogue size and expected monthly transfer to choose a plan. Track actual usage and support questions after launch, especially before a promotion.
## Before you publish
-
The product page matches the actual files, access and timing.
-
A sample or preview reflects the product customers receive.
-
The purchase, email and mobile access route have been tested.
-
Compatibility, licence terms and support contact are easy to find.
-
Capacity and costs have been checked against the current plan.
## Where BIG fits
BIG connects Shopify products with digital files, links and licence keys. It supports standard, custom and scheduled products. Paid plans include unlimited order counts, with plan-specific storage and bandwidth; API access and outgoing webhooks require Growth or Enterprise.
[Explore the features](https://www.bigdigitaldownload.com/features)
,
